Project principles - Zero OS

Zero and Ezero have different priorities. Zero grows the capabilities needed
by the system: explicit operations, device access, compiler targets and
reusable system facilities. Ezero builds on Zero and prioritizes convenience:
short programs, automatic value types and memory management, readable errors
and one-based collection indices. The operating system also prioritizes
simple defaults, automatic device setup and clear actions. These are design
priorities, not claims that every planned feature already exists.

Our own language is the foundation. Zero's data-first grammar has explicit
@references, => declarations, -> updates, ?/?? control forms and :/! operation
prefixes. Runtime sources use this grammar consistently. SYNTAX.ztext is the
language's source contract; changing syntax also changes compilers, sources,
documentation and meaningful regression tests together.

All active compiler and system implementation is Zero source. compiler.zero
compiles Zero, including its own source, and directly emits machine code for
Linux and Windows hosts, x86-64/IA32 kernels and native guest apps, both UEFI
ABIs and real-mode BIOS entry.
BIOS/UEFI loaders, the shared monolithic kernel, device drivers, filesystem,
terminal and system image builders are Zero source. Assembly and foreign
compiler backends are not part of the current source build.

Self-hosting is a reproducible build property: a Zero-built compiler compiles
compiler.zero, its result compiles the same source, and the executables must
match byte for byte. The executable seed has an explicit provenance. Our
earlier assembly-origin compiler was used for the one-time transition; that
history is retained honestly, not presented as an origin without assembly.

We implement source ourselves and emit native machine code directly. There
is no borrowed kernel, loader, driver implementation, arithmetic library,
guest runtime or compiler backend. Hardware standards and BIOS/UEFI/ELF/PE/
FAT/ISO9660/El Torito interfaces are external specifications, not inventions
we claim as our own. The visible terminal uses VGA text memory or our own
framebuffer renderer on a mode selected by VBE or GOP; PS/2, COM1 and ATA
protocol logic remains in Zero. Terminal window sessions, tiled and scrolling
layouts, labels and focus rendering are implemented by the kernel. No imported font or graphics library
supplies the desktop.

Multitasking has a concrete meaning in this release: terminal, graphics and
computation have independent native stacks and saved continuations. They
cooperate through explicit yields in our Zero scheduler. Their memory is
shared, and only one CPU executes them. This does not provide preemption,
process isolation, SMP or a guarantee against a worker that stops yielding.

The same kernel source builds for 32-bit and 64-bit x86 without weakening its
u64 disk-format semantics. BIOS chooses the appropriate mode from CPU
features; IA32 and AMD64 UEFI use their matching loaders. A missing long-mode
feature selects the 32-bit BIOS path. This release targets x86 PCs.

Behavior, limits and failures must be explicit. Files persist on disk and
writes are acknowledged only after flushing. Language tests, loader probes,
complete emulated boot, native VirtualBox operation and physical hardware
tests are separate forms of evidence. A VirtualBox firmware test under QEMU
is not a native VirtualBox test. A configured target is not proof of a boot.

System commands and diagnostics use English; the current desktop font and
keyboard use ASCII. Ezero source and text values support UTF-8, independently
of that desktop limitation. Human conversation language is independent of
the system language. User-visible lists and task identifiers start at1.

Disks have independent volume roots, assigned A: through Z:. GO selects a
root and the vertical bar separates path components: A:|notes. A volume is
not hidden as a directory inside another volume. Physical devices, partitions
and filesystems are separate concepts with separately checked boundaries.
Standard filesystems are compatibility formats, not code to import. Their
drivers are implemented in Zero; unsupported features receive explicit errors.

Build and validation boundary
All authored implementation, integration, building and checking code in this
project is Zero or Ezero. The original native builder performs compilation in
process, verifies self-reproduction and creates the images. The original
integrator composes source parts. The original verifier runs actual compiler,
frontend/runtime and host filesystem checks. No foreign-language project
program is renamed and presented as Zero.

Text documentation and manifests use our ZTEXT001 format and our reader.
Interoperability with existing hosts and firmware still requires their public
executable and boot interfaces. This does not make those hosts our operating
system. External virtual machines and independent inspectors provide testing
evidence and are kept outside the authored project. They are not shipped as
our implementation. A VM pass is not proof of physical hardware compatibility.
